    "title": "Who Made the Artemis II Rocket and Space Capsule? - The New York Times",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT See more of our coverage in your search results.Encuentra más de nuestra cobertura en los resultados de búsqueda. Add The New York Times on GoogleAgrega The New York Times en Google Space today seems dominated by billionaires — Elon Musk has SpaceX and Jeff Bezos owns Blue Origin, both rocket companies. But Artemis II is a throwback to an earlier era when NASA designed its spacecraft and then hired traditional aerospace companies to build them. Launch abort system Orion crew module The upper stage will burn the same liquid mixture of hydrogen and oxygen used in the Apollo mission. Upper stage RL-10 engine Liquid oxygen tank Liquid hydrogen tank Rocket boosters Core stage The engines on the core stage were built for the space shuttle program. Person to scale Shuttle RS-25 engines ROCKET BOTTOM VIEW Replaced engine Because of hydraulic leaks, NASA in 2025 replaced one engine with a spare intended for a future Artemis mission. Solid rocket booster Solid rocket booster Shuttle engines Launch abort system The upper stage will burn the same liquid mixture of hydrogen and oxygen used in the Apollo mission. Orion crew module Upper stage RL-10 engine Liquid oxygen tank Liquid hydrogen tank Rocket boosters Core stage The engines on the core stage were built for the space shuttle program. Person to scale Shuttle RS-25 engines ROCKET BOTTOM VIEW Replaced engine Solid rocket booster Solid rocket booster Shuttle engines Because of hydraulic leaks, NASA in 2025 replaced one engine with a spare intended for a future Artemis mission. Who Made the Artemis II Rocket and Space Capsule? - The New York Times The Space Launch System rocket, which is being used by Artemis II, is unusual in that Congress, frustrated when President Obama canceled an earlier return-to-the-moon program called Constellation, gave detailed specifications for what it wanted NASA to build. The pieces of S.L.S. are, in large part, a remix of components of the space shuttles. The Orion spacecraft has two main sections. The astronauts will remain inside the crew module during the 10-day mission. CREW MODULE The service module, a cylindrical piece provided by the European Space Agency, houses the propulsion, power and life-support systems. SERVICE MODULE Spacecraft adapter Panel covers CREW MODULE The Orion spacecraft has two main sections. The astronauts will remain inside the crew module during the 10-day mission. SERVICE MODULE The service module, a cylindrical piece provided by the European Space Agency, houses the propulsion, power and life-support systems. Spacecraft adapter Panel covers Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Kenneth Chang, a science reporter at The Times, covers NASA and the solar system, and research closer to Earth.Marco Hernandez is a Times graphics editor covering visually driven stories.See more on: National Aeronautics and Space AdministrationShare full articleRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTSupport independent journalism with a subscription. Already a subscriber? Log in.Enjoy unlimited access for $1 a week.Welcome offer$7.50 $1/weekBilled as $4 every four weeks for your first six months, then $30 thereafter. Continue with cardPayPalCancel or pause anytime.All Access includes news, plus Games, Cooking, Audio, Wirecutter and The Athletic.Offer for a New York Times All Access subscription. Current subscribers and those who have previously accepted a sale/introductory offer from The New York Times may be ineligible for another sale/introductory offer. Subscription excludes print edition. Some games may be available without a subscription. You will be automatically charged in advance $4.00 every 4 weeks for 6 months, and $30.00 every 4 weeks thereafter. Cancel before October 29, 2026 to avoid the transition to the standard price that will be charged on that date. You can cancel prior to renewal from your account settings online or during limited hours via chat or phone. Canc